// Floating point on words. A C double is carried as the 64 bits of its binary64
// (bits_of_float / float_of_bits move between that and an id float), and every
// operation here takes and answers such words, so crt and the generated code
// stay words throughout. A float is the same word holding a value that binary32
// represents; rounding to it is cfl_rnd32, and its 4-byte memory form is
// cfl_ld32 / cfl_st32. The cases spell the words as integers: 4609434218613702656
// is 1.5, 4612248968380809216 is 2.25, 4604930618986332160 is 0.75.
cfl_add(word a, word b) {
  float cfz = float_of_bits(a) + float_of_bits(b);
  word cfr = bits_of_float(cfz);
} return word cfr;
